+
+## Quality findings
+
+**Minor** — `PassContext::historyValid()`'s wrong-kind throw path (calling it on a non-history resource, `executor.cpp:1048-1051`) has no test. This codebase has an explicit, named precedent for exactly this class of coverage ("[Fix round 1, Minor finding, plus its supplemental follow-up covering the remaining three resolvers]: every PassContext resolver must throw std::out_of_range on a legitimately-registered but wrong-kind name" — `test_execute_gpu.cpp:895-913`, exercising `imageFormat()`/`image()`/`imageView()`/`buffer()`'s wrong-kind throws end-to-end through a real `Executor`). `historyValid()` is a fifth resolver added by this exact task and does not get the same treatment. Low risk (the throw itself was read and is correct — right exception type, names the resource, matches every sibling resolver's message shape) but a real, avoidable gap relative to this codebase's own established bar.
+
+**Minor / informational** — the canonical single-pass temporal-accumulator pattern (one pass declaring both `addHistoryInput` and `setHistoryOutput` for the same name, then calling `ctx.imageView("hist")` inside its own `execute()` callback to blend against its own previous-frame contents) is proven correct at compile time (`test_compile.cpp:1607-1646`: right access count, right execution order, no erroneous same-frame dependency) but is not exercised through an actual GPU submission — the GPU test's ping-pong proof uses two separate passes (`write_history`/`read_history`), not the fused single-pass form. The underlying code paths (`applyHistoryAccesses()`'s per-access resolution, `resolveImageView()`'s read-slot redirect) are generic and already proven independently on each half, so the residual risk is low, but this specific, likely-common usage shape has no direct empirical proof.
+
+No Critical, High, or other Medium findings. The core mechanism — ping-pong parity, cross-frame barrier persistence, first-frame contract, pinned/pooled isolation, and the bounds check — all check out against direct code trace, existing unit-test precedent for the reused primitives, and a fresh local re-run of every test the report cites.
